            <ArticleTitle>Angioplasty of a totally occluded right coronary artery.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>61-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We have presented a case of angioplasty of a chronically occluded right coronary artery. The occlusion had been present for 6 wks by clinical estimates. The length of the occluded segment (approximately 55 cm) did not preclude a successful outcome. Proper selection and manipulation of angioplasty equipment are, as in every case, critical for procedural success. Subintimal guidewire passage, though a frequent event, is occasionally associated with ischemic manifestations, and mandates detection and proper management. New approaches to PTCA of total coronary occlusions continue to be developed.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
